  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we are clearly never going to pay down our \ndebt, why are people still lending us money? And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well, what happens if they stop? In the past, this \nkind of question was basically dismissed by most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people in the no as a little more than an absurd \nhypothetical. But well, it seems these days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15800,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even the Treasury is kind of taking it seriously. \nLast month, our national debt passed $40 trillion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than doubling within the last decade \nalone. All fueled by a long trend of running  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26400,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "progressively bigger and bigger budget deficits. \nEven when adjusted for overall economic growth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are currently spending more and bringing in \nless than we have at any point in history outside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of major recessions, pandemics, and the Second \nWorld War. This recent track record, in addition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to massive new tax cuts and spending proposals, \nhas made it pretty clear that our government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't really have any intention of paying this \ndebt back or even really paying it down anymore.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51400,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make matters worse, other governments and even \nmajor corporations are now trying to attract the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same group of lenders, creating a bidding war, \npushing interest rates across the world to record  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60640,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highs. At the same time, debt is also at record \nhighs, meaning all of these borrowers need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrow more just to pay for their borrowing. It \nalso raises the question of if everybody is in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "record levels of debt, who is there actually \nleft to lend us this money? It's not great,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it's already causing some behaviors that \ncould really only be described as desperate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like declaring economic D-Day or using \nthe military to fight the bond markets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86640,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which isn't really a thing. But even outside of \ndirect military intervention in financial markets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91880,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever that might look like, the world's \ngovernments are still leaning more and more on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95560,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complicated financial trickery to keep things from \ngetting too bad. Which means unfortunately we all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to sit down and talk about the bond markets \nto find out what happens when those tricks stop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "working. The national debt just hit a record high \n40 trillion. [music] Yes, with a t dollars. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number staggering and it arrives sooner than \npredicted. The debt we believe that they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to uh continue to put the right policies in \nplace that will lead the yen to get [music] back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122200,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to more of a normal equilibrium price. We we are \ngoing to make a market the end. We routinely do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buybacks and we're going to increase the size \nof the buyback. Talking of China, [music] it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133360,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has cut its holdings of US Treasury bonds to \nthe lowest level since 2008. Even as global  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138120,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foreign ownerships of US debt hit a record high. \nWe have many types of intervention. That's one.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ultimate intervention is our military. \nAnd uh if we have to use that, we will. Yeah.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay. So, to understand why people seem to be \nfreaking out over bond markets all of the sudden,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154000,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm sorry, but you're going to need to understand \nhow bond markets are supposed to work so that you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can also understand how they could stop working. \nBecause of this, you are going to hear me say  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words like usually and typically and almost always \nand during normal times a lot for the next 5  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minutes. Partially, this is because bond markets \nare unintuitive even in those normal times,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also because, well, these aren't normal \ntimes. So, again, I'm sorry. Either way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182080,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since understanding how bonds work is basically \nunderstanding how money works, and well, you know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's why I'm here. Let's get into it. Okay, \nso to pay for everything the government does,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Treasury has the Treasury General Account. Our \ntax dollars go into this account and effectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything the federal government spends money \non comes out of it. It is for all intents and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202120,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purposes a national checking account. But we \nhaven't had nearly enough tax dollars coming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in to pay for the expenses coming out for more \nthan 20 years. So to keep enough money in this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211520,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account to in turn keep paying for everything \nthe government does, we need to borrow money.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The way we do that is that on a set schedule, we \nwill go to the market and offer to sell different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IUs with different maturities. We sell these \nIUs with payback terms ranging from 4 weeks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225960,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 30 years with a lot of options in between. \nA little side note is that this debt goes by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different names depending on that payback term. If \nthe Treasury will pay back the IUS in less than a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year, they are called Treasury bills. Between 2 \nand 10 years are Treasury notes. And then there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241160,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are 20 and 30-year terms that are called Treasury \nbonds. There are some minor differences in when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246720,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest is paid on [music] this debt. But for our \npurposes, when you hear treasury bills, notes, or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251320,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bonds, just remember that the only real difference \nis the payback period. Now, in a perfect world,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Treasury would love to borrow all of its \nmoney over a nice long 30-year time horizon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it doesn't do this for two reasons. The \nfirst is that not all potential lenders want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their money to be locked away for effectively half \na human lifetime. While it's true that if you buy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271040,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of these long-term bonds, you can sell them \nin secondary markets. This is surprisingly hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275320,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do because anybody looking to buy your bonds \nwould need a good reason not just to buy one of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the brand new ones that the Treasury is constantly \nissuing. And because we are constantly turnurning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284320,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out new ones at different times, and they stick \naround for decades, there are also just so many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different variations of these bonds. A bond \nwith 27 years left on it paying 5% interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293760,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is totally different from one paying 3% over \nthe next 22 years. So yeah, because of this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the secondary markets for long-term bonds are \nsurprisingly illquid for such a large asset class,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is actually kind of important to remember \nfor what's going on right now. So keep that in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308560,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mind for later. The other problem with really \nlong-term bonds is that they are also highly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "susceptible to interest rate changes. If you lend \nmoney to the US government at 3% for 30 years and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318160,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then interest rates climb to 6%, you will almost \ncertainly still get your money back plus the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322520,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest rate once your 30 years is up. But in the \nmeantime, your bond will be worth a lot less than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new one paying the higher interest rate. So if \nyou do need to sell it to free up some cash before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333360,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then, you will take a significant loss, especially \nearly on. A $1,000 bond paying 3% is $30 a year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if interest rates are 6%, someone could get \nthe same yearly return by only investing $500.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345800,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So your bond is naturally worth closer to this \namount with only the promise of a larger principal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repayment in the distant future to differentiate \nthem. As a not so fun fact, this is actually what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brought down Silicon Valley Bank. They put a lot \nof their reserves in longdated treasuries when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359840,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest rates were low at the start of the \npandemic. And then when interest rates rose,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363680,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the market value of those bonds fell, meaning \nthat their reserves were not worth enough to cover  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "withdrawals, even though in theory the money was \ninvested in one of the safest assets there are, on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373320,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paper at least. Now, I know this can be a little \nbit counterintuitive. But if you understand this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377920,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dynamic, then congratulations. You are apparently \ndoing better than the risk and financial officers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a major former financial institution. Either \nway, because of that ili liquidity as well as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388320,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "risk of inflation eating into real returns or even \njust the potential that the USA won't be around in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392840,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 years to honor this debt, longerterm treasuries \nalmost always sell at a premium. This is the yield  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398720,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curve that you might have heard people talking \nabout, which is really just a technical way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of saying that typically the shorter bonds pay \ninterest much closer to the floor set by the Fed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is also one of those unintuitive things \nbecause in our own personal lives, we typically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412440,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "associate short-term borrowing with higher \nrisk, higher interest consumer debt. But yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when lending to the government, it's the opposite. \nThis means theoretically it would almost always be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422480,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheaper to fund the government based on short-term \nborrowing because people are willing to accept  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower interest rates for those IUs. The problem \nwith that is if we just take out short-term debt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432120,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government constantly has to borrow new \nmoney to not only fund its fiscal shortfalls,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436400,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also cover the bills that are expiring. If \nit does this in short-term markets exclusively,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441640,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that means the rate of turnurning would \nincrease, and any increase in interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates would be immediately felt rather than being \nsoftly smoothed out over many years. Right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450080,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weighted average of all of the interest we \nare paying on our debt is actually around 3.1%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even though new treasuries are paying between \n3.8 8 and 5.3% interest. And that's because most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our debt pile is still from notes and bonds \nissued from many years which we took out back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when interest rates were much lower. If we relied \npurely on short-term borrowing, it would have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even cheaper back then. But our interest expense \ntoday would be double what it currently is.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This kind of cost predictability trade-off is \nwhy we offer so many different debt durations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It lets us get money from different groups of \nlenders. And we typically like to balance this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out so we have a good mix of long-term debt from a \nfinancing foundation and short-term debt to absorb  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any sudden movements in borrowing requirements. \nOkay. So now in the middle of this yield curve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the 10-year rate. And this is important for two \nreasons. For the government, this is the term over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which they do a bulk of their borrowing. But also \nthis is the benchmark by which most major consumer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505520,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lending is based. If a bank can get 4.8% 8% \ninterest lending to the government for 10 years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510760,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are going to want more out of you because \nthere is a much higher chance that you will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "default on your loan. Now, the reason that \nit's the 10-year that's particularly important  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not the 30-year, even though we do 30-year \nmortgages, is that almost nobody actually keeps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their mortgage for 30 years. People typically \nsell and refinance well before that. So, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average mortgage actually lasts about 10 years. \nAnd corporations also tend to borrow money around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this time frame as well as a nice happy medium. \nAgain, as a bit of foreshadowing, this is also why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government is kind of obsessed with bending \nthis curve to get that number as low as possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544320,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially around major political events like, oh, \nI don't know, something like midterm elections.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550120,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, now you know how the money markets work. It's \ntime to learn how money works to find out why the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money market suddenly uh isn't working. This \nvideo is sponsored by Flexis Spot and their E7  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pro standing desk. And if you spend all day at \na computer like I do, this is an upgrade that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565200,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually matters. The E7 Pro is their upgraded \ndual monitor standing desk with a 440 lb static  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571480,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "load capacity, a height range from 25 to 50.6 \nin, anti-colision protection, and a kid lock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also comes with a 15-year warranty and a 30-day \nrisk-free return window. My old desk always had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit of a shake under my setup, but the E7 Pro \nstays solid at any height, whether I am typing or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gaming, and switching between sitting and standing \nthrough the day has been a lot easier on my back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also sent over a C7 Max ergonomic chair. The \nheadrest, back rest height, seat depth, and 5D  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599440,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "armrests all adjust to fit you. The lumbar support \ncatches your lower back and sacrum instead of just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lumbar, and the high rebound and breathability \nof the mesh material holds up through long sits.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also comes with a 10-year warranty and a \n30-day return policy. Flexispot is running  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their Labor Day sale right now. Up to [music] \n65% off, plus extra discounts for students,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618960,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "teachers, medical workers, military, and recent \ngrads. [music] If you are on a tight budget,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check out the regular E7 desk or their C5 chair. \nAnd they just launched the C7 Morpher 2. Head to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629320,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flexispot.com and use my code HMW50 for $50 off \neither the E7 Pro desk or the C7 Max chair. And if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you grab one, leave a note with my channel name to \nhelp support the channel. Okay, so the bond market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has always relied on three assumptions. That \nAmerica will continue to make its repayments. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the American dollar it makes those repayments with \nwill still be worth something in the future. and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651040,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there are no better options out there to lend \nyour money to. The fact that yields are spiking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the world is generally an indication that \nthose assumptions are being a little less assumed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661000,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than they might have been previously. And there \nare a few reasons for this. The first is that a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot of the parties that typically lent money to us \nkind of need that money for themselves right now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around $8 trillion of our total outstanding debt \nis currently held up by the government itself in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a trust to finance various programs like social \nsecurity. This is often referred to as the money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government lends to itself, which is honestly \nneedlessly confusing because really it's money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it takes from paychecks to pay for programs \nthat it's obligated to cover. It just keeps the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffer in these bonds. But over time, as more \npeople are drawing from these programs than paying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695880,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into them, this buffer is shrinking, meaning \nthat one of the reliable buyers of government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700160,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debt has also been shrinking, even if it was \nkind of the government itself. On top of this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private pensions are also becoming far less common \nas most people have transitioned their retirement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planning to individual accounts. In the past, \ncorporations covering their employees pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payments were a big buyer of bonds, but obviously \nthat's become a smaller pool as well. Now, none of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is really a shock. We knew these variables \nwere coming for at least the last two decades,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there are now a few extra factors on top \nof it. Another major group thinking twice about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731040,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lending money to us right now is investors from \noverseas. Some of them can get higher yields from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736040,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own governments with just as good or even \nbetter borrowing safety without the currency risk.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other governments are also thinking twice about \nholding too much of their foreign reserves and US  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746160,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bonds because of [music] recent unpredictability \naround trade and the potential for sanctions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751880,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "China, in particular saw what we did to Russian \nbond holdings and decided to slowly pivot into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less sanctionable reserve assets, bringing their \nholdings down to an 18-year low. On top of this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of major corporations who have typically \nhad large cash reserves to keep in treasury bills  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are now using them to fund AI development or going \neven further and actually issuing their own bonds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771560,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which in theory compete with the bonds from the \nTreasury. In plain English, when the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "goes to auction off its IUs, there are fewer \nparties bidding on a greater number of lots, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781520,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of them have even gone so far as to actually \nstart selling their own IUs in the corner.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Less competition for the IUs on offer means the \nTreasury is forced to offer higher yields to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tempt the smaller group who's left to buy them. \nSo, the thing that made the headlines last week  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that Treasury Secretary Scott Bessant basically \ntried using some of the money in the Treasury  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "checking account to buy up some old long-term \nbonds to kind of act like another bidder to in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn try and make these long-term bonds seem \nmore attractive. He also tried using some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our spare cash to buy Japanese yen to maintain \nJapan's bidding power at this auction. All in an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attempt to get more parties bidding on long-term \nbonds to bring this side of the curve down a bit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would also hopefully bring the 10-year \ndown as well. In effect, the Treasury has kind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of resorted to day trading forex and bond markets \nto keep interest rates manageable, which is not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a great long-term strategy. Patrick Bole did an \nentire video on the futility of this little dance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so I will leave a link to that like always, but \nthe TLDDR is that it basically achieved nothing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After a small dip, rates went right back up again. \nNow again, by itself, this wouldn't necessarily be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a problem. With inflation still running higher \nthan ideal, slightly elevated interest rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can help to control demand as a bitter but \narguably necessary medicine. Yes, in theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government is going to pay more in interest \nevery year. But in practice, the government is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not a monolith. It's made up of people with \ntheir own objectives. And realistically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the current administration doesn't like this \nbecause higher borrowing costs for the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means higher borrowing costs for businesses and \nvoters. They are clearly trying to fix this, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in turn might ironically be making the problem \nworse. There are only four ways this debt will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881920,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever be resolved. The first is that we grow our \neconomy and ultimately our tax base faster than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the debt grows. This is the ideal scenario, and \nit's clearly what is being pitched as a solution.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892800,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's like someone with $30,000 in credit card \ndebt. On minimum wage, that is a crippling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem. But if they somehow land a job making six \nfigures, it's far more manageable. The problem is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now we have so much debt that the government is on \ntrack to spend more than a trillion dollars this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908120,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year just on interest payments alone. Meaning that \na lot of that growth is basically only keeping up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912640,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with interest. And the higher the interest rate \nis, the more we need to grow just to offset it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917240,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is not helped by our aging demographics, \nwhich means fewer workers paying taxes and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more old people claiming benefits. That's not to \nmention the fact that we are actually lowering the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tax base with cuts from the one big beautiful \nbill. So, we would need to grow even faster to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offset the interest and the shrinking tax rate. \nSo, while it would be nice to rely on this alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938800,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most potential lenders realize it's probably \nnot going to happen. The second slightly less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appealing option is that we start taxing more \nand spending less and run a fiscal surplus or,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you know, just a slightly less gaping deficit \nto pay down the debt. It seems reasonable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but a lot of our spending commitments are \nnon-discretionary. and what big spending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "categories we can control like the military are \nactually being increased. And again on the other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side we have cut taxes especially on high-income \nearners and businesses. Now there is always the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "argument that even extreme taxes on wealthy people \nwouldn't go very far to covering this debt. But uh  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974520,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are some problems with this logic. It's true \nthat our debt has grown to levels that are going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be hard to deal with quickly. But the thing is \nwho do you think is lending us this money in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first place? The majority of our lending is funded \nby domestic lenders who are mostly wealthy people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989520,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with money market accounts, direct bond holdings \nor indirectly through their banks. Regardless of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the technicalities, they have enough money to \nfund the government through bond issuances. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they also have enough money to fund the government \nthrough taxes. It's just that obviously one is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004840,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not quite as appealing. Thought exercises aside \nthough, if fiscal responsibility was the hope,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are clearly not heading in the right direction. \nThe third even less appealing option is just to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflate away the debt, get the Fed to lower \nrates, crank up that money printer, put money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into circulation, and pay the whole thing off in \n5 years with a single crisp $40 trillion note,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which at the time would be enough to buy about 8 \nGB of RAM. Obviously, that is an extreme scenario,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the market is kind of already pricing in a \nmuch more toned down version. Inflation is already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037920,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up due to tariffs in the war in Iran. spending is \nnot slowing and even if nobody else is willing to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043960,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lend the government money to roll over their debt, \nthe Fed won't realistically let the US government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "default. So, they'll create dollars to buy this \ndebt, but those dollars it makes will just end up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053520,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being worth less. Not necessarily worthless, but \nworth less, if you get what I mean. The problem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is since lenders are already anticipating this, \nthey want a higher interest rate to offset the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation risk, which in turn makes it even harder \nto grow or tax their way out of this, meaning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation is more likely, meaning even higher \ninterest rates to offset the inflation risk.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Short-term rates will ultimately closely follow \nthe Fed's target rate. But long-term lenders might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually see an interest rate cut as capitulation \nto inflation and end up demanding even higher  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
